---
title: "CI incident: the evaluator patch outran the daemon protocol"
date: 2026-07-09
---

Required `flake-check` jobs began failing with unbuilt
`cargo-unit-test-manifest` outputs. The same logs also contained SQLite
`database is busy` warnings, and a live daemon experiment started just
before the failures. Both were plausible causes. Neither was the root
protocol incompatibility.

## Root cause

The repo's patched `nix-eval-jobs` resolves floating content-addressed
outputs by calling `store.queryRealisation(id)`. The call itself is
fine; the problem was a client/daemon mismatch. The evaluator's Nix
libraries had drifted to a Nix-master component set whose
`queryRealisation` client demands the `realisation-with-path-not-hash`
protocol feature, while the shared CI daemon runs the Nix 2.34 family,
which does not advertise it. With mismatched families, evaluation
cannot obtain the manifest output path.

A Nix `2.36.0pre20260708_ac94798` experiment from `/tmp/nix-fixed` had
replaced the deployed daemon at 01:03:33 UTC. After its active builds
finished, the host was restored to the Nix 2.34.7 system service and the
store endpoint was verified. Fresh CI still printed the missing-feature
warning and failed on the same manifest. The experiment explained the
host drift, but both daemon builds lacked the feature the evaluator
patch assumed.

## Differentials

- PR #2600 passed `eval-cache=false` to both evaluator phases. SQLite
  warnings disappeared, while the manifest failure remained.
- Restoring the deployed Nix 2.34.7 daemon removed the live experiment,
  while the missing protocol feature and manifest failure remained.
- The failure site is the patched evaluator's `store.queryRealisation`
  call, not the manifest builder itself.

## Status

- PR #2600 fixed parallel evaluator cache contention.
- Issue #2592 is closed. PR #2606 relinked `nix-eval-jobs` against the
  daemon's pinned Nix 2.34 component set
  (`packages/nix-eval-jobs/default.nix`), with a smoke test that
  fails the build if the evaluator ever drifts off the daemon's family
  again.
- CI evaluates content-addressed derivations again. The durable fix was
  aligning the client with the deployed daemon protocol, not upgrading
  the daemon.

